{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 29, :comments [{:quotes [{:he "חמה מימיכם ומשמאלם", :translit "chahmah miyeminam umisemolam", :gloss "a wall on their right and on their left"} {:he "חמה כתיב חסר", :translit "chamah katuv chaser", :gloss "chamah is written defective"} {:he "חמה", :translit "chamah", :gloss "wrath"} {:he "פסל מיכה", :translit "pesel michah", :gloss "idol of Micah"} {:he "ועבר בים צרה", :translit "ve'avar bayam tzarah", :gloss "and He passed through the sea of distress"} {:he "ס\"ת רמה", :translit "sof teivot ramah", :gloss "end-letters spell ramah"} {:he "פסל מיכה", :translit "pesel michah", :gloss "idol of Micah"}], :mechanisms [{:kind :gematria, :a "רמה", :b "פסל מיכה"} {:kind :sofei-tevot, :phrase "ועבר בים צרה", :spells "רמה"}], :frame {:text "A tradition preserved in Rabbinic sources holds that the idol of Micah (Judges 17–18) was carried by the tribe of Dan through the splitting of the Sea of Reeds (Mechilta d'Rabbi Yishmael, Beshalach; Sanhedrin 103b). The word חמה written defective (without its expected vav/waw) signals divine wrath aroused by this idol's presence among Israel at the sea. Isaiah 63:13 describes God leading Israel through the depths; its phrase וַיַּנְחֵם לִרְדּוֹת בַּתְּהֹמוֹת is connected to the sea crossing. The word רמה derived from the final letters of ועבר בים צרה (Isaiah 8:8 or similar) equals פסל מיכה in gematria.", :sources ["Judges 17–18" "Mechilta d'Rabbi Yishmael Beshalach" "Sanhedrin 103b" "Isaiah 8:8"]}}], :arithmetic [{:kind :gematria, :a "רמה", :a-value 245, :b "פסל מיכה", :b-value 245, :lens :standard, :by :selah, :equal? true} {:kind :sofei-tevot, :phrase "ועבר בים צרה", :spells "רמה", :letters ["ר" "ם" "ה"], :by :selah, :order :in-order}], :book :exodus, :voice :baal-haturim, :lang :en, :chapter 14, :discipline "bht-chalkboard-v1", :timestamp "2026-07-13T00:33:10.280909845Z", :model "glm-5.2"}